    "workedExample": {
      "title": "Worked UK VAT Transaction Scenarios",
      "html": "<div class=\"space-y-6\"><div class=\"p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 bg-slate-50/50 dark:bg-slate-800/30\"><h4 class=\"text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-2\">Scenario A: B2B Consulting Invoice (VAT Exclusive +20%)</h4><p class=\"text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3\">A consultancy bills £4,500.00 net for services rendered. Adding 20% standard VAT (£4,500 × 0.20 = £900.00) produces a total client invoice of £5,400.00. The business records £900.00 as output tax to remit on its next MTD return.</p></div><div class=\"p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 bg-slate-50/50 dark:bg-slate-800/30\"><h4 class=\"text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-2\">Scenario B: Retail Store Purchase (VAT Inclusive Reverse Extraction)</h4><p class=\"text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3\">A company purchases IT office hardware at retail for £1,440.00 inclusive of VAT. Net equipment cost: £1,440 ÷ 1.20 = £1,200.00. Embedded VAT: £1,440 - £1,200 = £240.00 (which equals £1,440 ÷ 6). The VAT-registered company reclaims £240.00 as input tax from HMRC.</p></div><div class=\"p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 bg-slate-50/50 dark:bg-slate-800/30\"><h4 class=\"text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-2\">Scenario C: Reduced Rate Energy Supply (5% VAT)</h4><p class=\"text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3\">A residential utility bill comes to £300.00 net fuel consumption. Subject to 5% reduced VAT (£300 × 0.05 = £15.00), total payable is £315.00. If extracting from a £315 inclusive bill: £315 ÷ 1.05 = £300 net, with £15 tax portion.</p></div></div>"
    },
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is the mandatory UK VAT registration threshold?",
        "answer": "The statutory UK VAT registration threshold is £90,000. You must register for VAT with HMRC if your total taxable turnover over the prior 12 rolling months exceeds £90,000, or if you expect your turnover to exceed £90,000 in the next 30 days alone."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you calculate and extract VAT from a gross inclusive price?",
        "answer": "To extract 20% VAT from a gross inclusive figure, divide the gross amount by 1.20 to find the net price, then subtract that net price from the gross total. Shortcut: dividing any 20% VAT-inclusive total by 6 yields the exact VAT component."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the difference between zero-rated supplies and VAT-exempt supplies?",
        "answer": "Zero-rated goods (such as books and basic groceries) are taxable at 0% VAT, meaning businesses do not charge customers tax but can still reclaim input VAT on related business expenses. VAT-exempt goods (such as health services and finance) carry no VAT, and businesses cannot reclaim input tax on costs incurred to provide them."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is Making Tax Digital (MTD) for VAT?",
        "answer": "Making Tax Digital (MTD) is HMRC's regulatory requirement that all VAT-registered businesses maintain digital accounting records and submit their quarterly VAT returns directly through MTD-compatible software using HMRC's secure API."
      },
      {
        "question": "How does the HMRC Flat Rate Scheme operate for small businesses?",
        "answer": "The Flat Rate Scheme allows small businesses with turnover up to £150,000 to pay a fixed percentage of gross turnover to HMRC based on business sector (ranging from 4% to 16.5%), while retaining the standard 20% collected from clients without reclaiming itemized purchase VAT."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can non-UK businesses claim back UK VAT incurred on business travel?",
        "answer": "Yes. Non-UK businesses can reclaim VAT incurred in the UK on qualifying business expenses (such as hotels, exhibition fees, and trade conferences) under HMRC Notice 723A, provided their home country does not require local establishment."
      }
    ],
